PG.notes("l201", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains that Node is a general-purpose JavaScript runtime while Express is a web framework layered on top of it, built specifically for server back ends. Shows why Express replaces hand-written Node server code in almost every real project.</p>\n<h3>Runtime vs framework</h3>\n<ul><li>Node.js is strictly a runtime environment: it lets JavaScript run on a computer outside the browser. It is not a framework.</li><li>Express is a JavaScript framework that sits on top of Node and is aimed at one job, building the back end of a website.</li><li>Professional teams normally combine the two: Node supplies the runtime, Express supplies the structure for handling web requests.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why Express is needed</h3>\n<ul><li>Node is general purpose. Beyond web back ends it is used for IoT devices and desktop apps (VS Code runs on Electron, which embeds Node), so it has no web-specific conveniences built in.</li><li>Express is a specialised tool for the web-server case: an electric screwdriver versus a plain one. Both can do the job; the specialised tool is faster for that one task.</li><li>A two-page site (home and about) written in bare Node needs a lot of low-level code that is hard to read. The same site in Express is short, modular and easy to follow, because Express talks to Node's internals for you.</li><li>Listed advantages: better readability, less code to write, and middleware, which lets you plug in features one piece at a time (covered in a later lesson).</li></ul>\n<h3>Popularity</h3>\n<ul><li>Express is the default choice among JavaScript back-end frameworks, and State of JS usage statistics back this up.</li><li>That is why the course skips building a server with raw Node and goes straight to Express.</li></ul>\n<h3><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> What Express removes</h3>\n<ul><li>In bare Node you create a server with http.createServer, then branch on req.url and req.method by hand, set the status and Content-Type headers yourself with res.writeHead, and call res.end.</li><li>Express replaces that branching with one method call per route (app.get(path, handler)), sets sensible headers automatically in res.send, and returns a 404 'Cannot GET /path' page for unmatched routes without any extra code.</li><li>Express is itself built on Node's http module: app.listen creates an http.Server under the hood.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The same two-page site in bare Node (no Express), for contrast</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import http from &quot;node:http&quot;;\n\nconst server = http.createServer((req, res) =&gt; {\n  if (req.method === &quot;GET&quot; &amp;&amp; req.url === &quot;/&quot;) {\n    res.writeHead(200, { &quot;Content-Type&quot;: &quot;text/html&quot; });\n    res.end(&quot;&lt;h1&gt;Home&lt;/h1&gt;&quot;);\n  } else if (req.method === &quot;GET&quot; &amp;&amp; req.url === &quot;/about&quot;) {\n    res.writeHead(200, { &quot;Content-Type&quot;: &quot;text/html&quot; });\n    res.end(&quot;&lt;h1&gt;About&lt;/h1&gt;&quot;);\n  } else {\n    res.writeHead(404, { &quot;Content-Type&quot;: &quot;text/plain&quot; });\n    res.end(&quot;Not found&quot;);\n  }\n});\n\nserver.listen(3000);</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The same site in Express (built in the next two lessons)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\nconst app = express();\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.send(&quot;&lt;h1&gt;Home&lt;/h1&gt;&quot;);\n});\n\napp.get(&quot;/about&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.send(&quot;&lt;h1&gt;About&lt;/h1&gt;&quot;);\n});\n\napp.listen(3000);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Calling Node a framework. It is a runtime; Express is the framework.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Assuming Express is part of Node. It is a separate npm package that must be installed.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l202", "<p class=\"summary\">Walks through the six steps to build and start a minimal Express server: project folder, index.js, npm init, installing express, writing the listen code and running it with node. Explains localhost, ports, how to see which ports are in use, and why the first visit to the server shows a Cannot GET / error.</p>\n<h3>Back-end vocabulary recap</h3>\n<ul><li>A server is a computer that stays on and waits for requests for resources such as HTML, CSS or JavaScript files. The back end is that computer plus the application running on it (for example an index.js) and, in bigger sites, a database.</li><li>A client, usually a user's browser, sends a request across the internet to the server. The application finds the resource and sends it back.</li><li>Client-side means the user-facing front end. Server-side means everything on the back end; people often shorten this to just 'the server'.</li><li>In this lesson the developer's own machine acts as the server. It only needs to be on while testing, not 24/7.</li></ul>\n<h3>The six steps</h3>\n<ul><li>1. Create a project directory. 2. Create index.js inside it. 3. Run npm init. 4. Install the express package. 5. Write the server code in index.js. 6. Start it with node and send it a request.</li><li>The terminal commands are assumed knowledge from the command line module: cd moves into a folder, mkdir creates one, touch creates an empty file. Tab completes names.</li><li>A folder name with spaces must be wrapped in quotes in mkdir, otherwise the shell treats each word as a separate argument.</li></ul>\n<h3>npm setup</h3>\n<ul><li>npm init -y creates package.json using defaults, skipping the interactive questions.</li><li>The main field in package.json must point at the entry file. The default is index.js, so name the file index.js (a different name like app.js would not match the default).</li><li>npm i express installs the package (short for install; check the spelling). This creates node_modules containing express and all its dependencies, and adds an express entry under dependencies in package.json (package-lock.json is also generated).</li><li>Version numbers differ over time (4.x, 5.x); the code works the same.</li><li>Add &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot; to package.json so that ES module syntax (import/export) works.</li></ul>\n<h3>The server code line by line</h3>\n<ul><li>import express from &quot;express&quot; loads the framework (lowercase e is the variable name).</li><li>const app = express() calls the imported function to create the application object. Everything else hangs off app.</li><li>const port = 3000 as a top-level constant keeps the port in one place; reuse it wherever needed rather than hard-coding the number.</li><li>app.listen(port, callback) takes two arguments: the port to listen on and a callback that runs once the server is up and ready.</li><li>The callback just logs a message such as 'Server running on port 3000.' using a template literal (backticks) so the port variable is interpolated.</li><li>No explicit Node code appears: Express handles the low-level parts.</li><li>Start the server with node index.js in the terminal. The log line proves it is running.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> express is a factory function, not a class, so it is called without new. Each call returns a fresh app object with methods such as get, post, use and listen.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> app.listen(port, callback) wraps Node's http.createServer(app).listen(port) and returns the underlying http.Server. The callback fires on the 'listening' event, i.e. only once the port is actually bound.</li></ul>\n<h3>localhost and ports</h3>\n<ul><li>localhost means 'this computer'. Visiting http://localhost:3000 sends the request to your own machine instead of a remote server.</li><li>A port is like a numbered door into the computer. (Extra) Port numbers run from 0 to 65535; each service listens on its own number so that services do not interfere (a presentation remote app, a printer and the Express app can each use a different one).</li><li>The number after the colon in the URL selects which port to reach. Here the server listens on 3000, so the URL must use :3000.</li><li>To see which ports are listening: on macOS run sudo lsof -i -P -n | grep LISTEN (needs your login password); on Windows run netstat -ano | findstr &quot;LISTENING&quot;. The | symbol is the pipe, which sends one command's output into the next. The Node process appears against port 3000.</li><li>While the server runs it occupies the terminal, so there is no prompt. Stop it with Ctrl+C, or open a second terminal with the + button to keep it running.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> localhost resolves to the loopback address 127.0.0.1 (IPv6 ::1), so the request never leaves the machine. Ports below 1024 usually need admin rights, which is one reason dev servers use numbers like 3000.</li></ul>\n<h3>First response: Cannot GET /</h3>\n<ul><li>Visiting localhost:3000 shows an error saying Cannot GET /, because the server is running but has no route that answers a GET request for the home page yet.</li><li>Fixing this requires understanding HTTP requests and defining route handlers, covered in the next lesson.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Terminal setup commands</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">cd Backend\nmkdir &quot;3.1 Express Server&quot;\ncd &quot;3.1 Express Server&quot;\ntouch index.js\nnpm init -y\nnpm i express</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">package.json after setup (key parts)</p><pre data-lang=\"json\">{\n  &quot;main&quot;: &quot;index.js&quot;,\n  &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot;,\n  &quot;dependencies&quot;: {\n    &quot;express&quot;: &quot;^5.1.0&quot;\n  }\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">index.js: minimal Express server</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\n\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\n\napp.listen(port, () =&gt; {\n  console.log(`Server running on port ${port}.`);\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Listing listening ports</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\"># macOS\nsudo lsof -i -P -n | grep LISTEN\n# Windows\nnetstat -ano | findstr &quot;LISTENING&quot;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Build and run your first Express server</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> An empty folder inside your Web Development Projects folder, VS Code open with its terminal.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Create a working Express project that starts on port 3000 and can be reached at localhost:3000.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Open the terminal and cd to the folder that will hold the project.</li><li>mkdir a new project folder (quote the name if it has spaces) and cd into it.</li><li>touch index.js to create the entry file.</li><li>Run npm init -y, then npm i express.</li><li>Add &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot; to package.json.</li><li>In index.js import express, create app, define a port constant and call app.listen with a logging callback.</li><li>Run node index.js and confirm the log message appears.</li><li>Open http://localhost:3000 in a browser and observe the Cannot GET / message.</li><li>Optionally run the lsof or netstat command in a second terminal to find the Node process on port 3000, then stop the server with Ctrl+C.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Terminal prints the running message; the browser shows Cannot GET /, proving the server answered but has no route yet.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\n\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\n\napp.listen(port, () =&gt; {\n  console.log(`Server running on port ${port}.`);\n});</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Unquoted folder names containing spaces in mkdir.</li><li>Naming the entry file something other than index.js while package.json main still says index.js.</li><li>Forgetting &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot;: older Node versions throw 'Cannot use import statement outside a module'. (Extra) Recent Node versions may detect the ES module syntax and run anyway with a warning, but setting the field explicitly is still the correct fix.</li><li>Typing the wrong package name when installing.</li><li>Running the server in the only terminal and then expecting a prompt; use a second terminal or Ctrl+C.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Starting a second copy while one is still running fails with an address-already-in-use error on port 3000.</li><li>Reference repo note: the repo's package.json carries the author's own name and extra metadata, which can be ignored.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l203", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces HTTP and its five main request verbs (GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE), then adds app.get route handlers to the Express server so it responds instead of showing Cannot GET. Covers req and res, rawHeaders, res.send with text and HTML, the address-in-use error, nodemon, and endpoints, ending with a challenge to serve /, /contact and /about.</p>\n<h3>What HTTP is</h3>\n<ul><li>HTTP is HyperText Transfer Protocol: the agreed language that lets computers talk to each other over the internet.</li><li>Five request verbs cover most needs: GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE.</li></ul>\n<h3>The five verbs</h3>\n<ul><li>GET: ask the server for a resource (an HTML page, text, or data). A browser address-bar visit is a GET request. This is the verb named in the Cannot GET error.</li><li>POST: send a resource to the server, for example a sign-up form submitting email and password. The server then processes or stores it (database, etc.).</li><li>PUT: update by completely replacing the existing resource with the one sent.</li><li>PATCH: update by changing only part of the resource. A product-return comparison helps: PUT is a whole replacement item, PATCH is a single replacement part.</li><li>DELETE: ask the server to remove a resource from the server or database.</li></ul>\n<h3>Handling a GET route in Express</h3>\n<ul><li>app.get(path, callback) tells the server what to do when a GET request arrives at that path.</li><li>The path &quot;/&quot; is the root, the home page. Visiting localhost:3000 with or without a trailing slash targets it.</li><li>The callback receives two parameters, conventionally req (the incoming request) and res (the response to send back). Inside the callback the code decides the reply.</li><li>console.log(req) prints the whole request object, which is huge. req.rawHeaders narrows it to the request headers: a flat array that alternates header name and value (for example 'Host', 'localhost:3000', 'User-Agent', '...Chrome...'), revealing the host, the client application, the operating system and other details. The log appears in the server's terminal, not the browser console.</li><li>res.send(body) sends a response back to the browser and ends the request. A plain string appears as text; a string of HTML such as &quot;&lt;h1&gt;Hello&lt;/h1&gt;&quot; is rendered as formatted HTML.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> When given a string, res.send sets the Content-Type header to text/html, which is why HTML markup in the string is rendered rather than shown as text. It also sets Content-Length and finishes the response, so call it only once per request.</li><li>Route code is registered at startup, so changing it requires restarting the server unless a watcher is used.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Routes are matched in the order they are registered; for an unmatched path Express falls through to its built-in 404 handler, which produces the 'Cannot GET /path' page.</li></ul>\n<h3>Restarting and the address-in-use error</h3>\n<ul><li>With plain node, every code change needs Ctrl+C then node index.js again.</li><li>Running a second server on port 3000 while the first is alive gives an 'address already in use' error because the port is occupied. Fix it by stopping the first process (go to its terminal and Ctrl+C), or by killing whatever holds the port with a terminal command (differs for Mac, Windows and Linux).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> On macOS you can find the process id with lsof -i :3000 and stop it with kill &lt;pid&gt;.</li></ul>\n<h3>nodemon</h3>\n<ul><li>nodemon watches your files and automatically restarts the Node app whenever they change.</li><li>Install it globally with npm i -g nodemon (-g means global, available to all projects, not just one). If the install fails, copy the command from the nodemon docs. (Extra) On macOS a permissions error may need sudo npm i -g nodemon; alternatively npx nodemon index.js runs it without a global install.</li><li>Run it by replacing node with nodemon: nodemon index.js.</li><li>It restarts on save, so you only need to refresh the browser to see the change.</li></ul>\n<h3>Endpoints</h3>\n<ul><li>An endpoint is the path part of the URL after the domain, such as /about or /contact. A letter addressed to a destination is the comparison used for it.</li><li>Each endpoint you want to support gets its own app.get call with its own handler. Different paths return different content.</li><li>This is the basis for multi-page sites and navigation built on a Node and Express back end.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Server with one GET route</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  console.log(req.rawHeaders);\n  res.send(&quot;&lt;h1&gt;Hello&lt;/h1&gt;&quot;);\n});\n\napp.listen(port, () =&gt; {\n  console.log(`Server running on port ${port}.`);\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Install and run with nodemon</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">npm i -g nodemon\nnodemon index.js</pre>\n<h2>The task: 3.2 HTTP Requests: new server with /, /contact and /about</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Nothing: a new folder (3.2 HTTP Requests) in the Backend module. Part one repeats the six setup steps from the previous lesson; part two adds routes.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> A server that answers GET requests at the home page, /contact and /about with different HTML content.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create the project folder and index.js, cd into it.</li><li>Run npm init -y and npm i express.</li><li>Set &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot; in package.json.</li><li>In index.js import express, create app, set port 3000 and call app.listen with a logging callback.</li><li>Run it (node index.js or nodemon index.js) and confirm Cannot GET / still shows.</li><li>Add app.get(&quot;/&quot;, ...) that sends an h1 for the home page.</li><li>Copy the get call twice for &quot;/contact&quot; and &quot;/about&quot;. Send an h1 plus a paragraph in each (a heading and a phone number for contact, a heading and a name for about).</li><li>Save, refresh the browser at the root, /contact and /about to check each page.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Each URL shows its own content and the Cannot GET error is gone for those three paths. Any other path still returns Cannot GET.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.send(&quot;&lt;h1&gt;Hello&lt;/h1&gt;&quot;);\n});\n\napp.get(&quot;/contact&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.send(&quot;&lt;h1&gt;Contact me&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;Phone: 0123 456 789&lt;/p&gt;&quot;);\n});\n\napp.get(&quot;/about&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.send(&quot;&lt;h1&gt;About me&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;My name is Angela.&lt;/p&gt;&quot;);\n});\n\napp.listen(port, () =&gt; {\n  console.log(`Server running on port ${port}.`);\n});</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting to restart the server after editing routes when not using nodemon.</li><li>Running two servers on port 3000 at once.</li><li>Forgetting &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot; in package.json.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Not sending a response from a handler, leaving the browser waiting until it times out (every route should call res.send or similar).</li><li>Reference repo note: it follows the same layout but replaces the contact and about content with the repo author's personal details and puts app.listen before the routes; both orderings work.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Editing the file while running plain node and refreshing the browser: the old code keeps serving until the process is restarted.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l204", "<p class=\"summary\">Covers HTTP response status codes and their five families, then shows the Express methods for POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE using res.sendStatus. Introduces Postman for testing non-GET routes without building a front end, with an exercise that exercises all five verbs against a provided server.</p>\n<h3>Request and response flow</h3>\n<ul><li>The client (desktop, laptop or phone) sends a request to the server side, which includes the machine, the application (index.js) and any database. The server sends a response back.</li><li>A response can carry text or HTML, but it can also be just a status code.</li></ul>\n<h3>HTTP status code families</h3>\n<ul><li>Codes are three-digit numbers, grouped by first digit (MDN documents them at developer.mozilla.org under Web/HTTP/Status).</li><li>100s: informational. 200s: success. 300s: redirection. 400s: client error. 500s: server error.</li><li>A memory aid: 1xx means wait or here is some info, 2xx means success, 3xx means go elsewhere, 4xx means the client made a mistake, 5xx means the server has a fault (bug, missing resource).</li><li>201 Created: a new resource was created, typical reply to a POST. (Extra) A 201 response may still carry a body or a Location header pointing at the new resource; in this lesson it is sent with no content.</li><li>201 Created: a new resource was created, typical after a POST (or PUT) where nothing needs to be sent back.</li><li>301: the resource moved permanently and the response gives the new URL. A mistyped domain that redirects to the right site shows this in the browser dev tools Network tab (tick Preserve log, filter All).</li><li>404: the server cannot find the requested resource, usually a wrong URL.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> 200 OK is the general-purpose success code; 201 is specific to creation, so do not treat it as the default success reply.</li></ul>\n<h3>Other HTTP methods in Express</h3>\n<ul><li>Express has one method per verb: app.get, app.post, app.put, app.patch and app.delete. Each takes a path and a (req, res) callback, just like app.get.</li><li>POST requests usually originate from an HTML form (sign-up page) carrying data in the request body. The server may validate or store it, then replies with 200 plus data or 201 with nothing.</li><li>res.sendStatus(code) replies with just a status code (and its standard text) instead of a body. Use it when the route has no content to return.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> res.sendStatus(code) sets the status and sends the code's standard reason phrase as a plain-text body (201 gives 'Created', 200 gives 'OK'). It is shorthand for res.status(code).send(text). To send a custom body with a status, chain res.status(201).send(...) or res.status(201).json(...).</li><li>In a browser address bar only GET routes can be tested, since typing a URL always makes a GET request. The other verbs need a form or a testing tool.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Express does not parse a request body by default: inside these handlers req.body is undefined until a body-parsing middleware such as express.urlencoded({ extended: true }) is added (covered in the middleware lessons). Body fields sent from Postman are therefore ignored here.</li></ul>\n<h3>Postman</h3>\n<ul><li>Postman is a free desktop API client (postman.com/downloads) for sending HTTP requests to a server without writing a front end. Developers use it to test routes before the front end exists.</li><li>Each request tab has a method dropdown (GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE), a URL field for the host, port and endpoint, and a Body tab for sending data, such as x-www-form-urlencoded key and value pairs like name and email.</li><li>Click Send to fire the request. The response status code, time, size and body appear below; hovering over the status explains its meaning.</li><li>Connection refused means nothing is listening on that port, i.e. the server is not running.</li><li>PUT is meant to replace a whole resource, so the client would normally send the complete data. PATCH sends only the field(s) to change. DELETE needs just the right path.</li><li>Real handlers would update or delete data; in this lesson the routes only return status codes to demonstrate the mechanics.</li></ul>\n<h3>Running a downloaded Node project</h3>\n<ul><li>Downloaded projects normally omit node_modules because it is large. cd into the project folder and run npm install with no package names; it reads package.json and installs every listed dependency.</li><li>Then start the server with nodemon index.js.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Routes for each verb, returning status codes (from the lesson project)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.send(&quot;&lt;h1&gt;Hello World&lt;/h1&gt;&quot;);\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/register&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.sendStatus(201);\n});\n\napp.put(&quot;/user/angela&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.sendStatus(200);\n});\n\napp.patch(&quot;/user/angela&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.sendStatus(200);\n});\n\napp.delete(&quot;/user/angela&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.sendStatus(200);\n});\n\napp.listen(port, () =&gt; {\n  console.log(`Server running on port ${port}.`);\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Set up the downloaded project</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">cd &quot;3.3 Postman&quot;\nnpm install\nnodemon index.js</pre>\n<h2>The task: 3.3 Postman: test all five verbs</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Download and extract the 3.3 Postman zip. It holds an Express server with a GET route at the root, POST /register, and PUT, PATCH and DELETE routes on a user path, all returning status codes. Postman installed.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Create one Postman tab per HTTP method and confirm each returns the expected success status code.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>cd into the extracted project and run npm install to fetch dependencies.</li><li>Start the server with nodemon index.js.</li><li>In Postman create a GET request to localhost:3000 (the trailing slash is optional) and Send. Expect the h1 body and status 200.</li><li>Create a POST to localhost:3000/register; optionally add urlencoded body fields. Expect 201 Created.</li><li>Create a PUT to localhost:3000/user/angela (the path must match the one in index.js exactly). Expect 200 OK.</li><li>Create a PATCH to the same user path, optionally with one changed field in the body. Expect 200.</li><li>Create a DELETE to the same path. Expect 200.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Try a wrong path or method (for example a POST to /user/angela/x) to see Express's 404 'Cannot POST ...' response.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Five Postman tabs, each returning the correct status (200, 201, 200, 200, 200), with bodies '&lt;h1&gt;Hello World&lt;/h1&gt;', 'Created', 'OK', 'OK', 'OK'. With the server stopped, Postman shows connection refused.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">// Routes added to the server for each non-GET verb\napp.post(&quot;/register&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.sendStatus(201);\n});\n\napp.put(&quot;/user/angela&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.sendStatus(200);\n});\n\napp.patch(&quot;/user/angela&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.sendStatus(200);\n});\n\napp.delete(&quot;/user/angela&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.sendStatus(200);\n});</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Testing POST, PUT, PATCH or DELETE by typing a URL in the browser; that always sends GET and gives Cannot GET or the wrong route.</li><li>Using a path in Postman that does not match the one in the server code (the username segment must match), giving 404.</li><li>Forgetting npm install after downloading a project, so express is missing.</li><li>Connection refused because the server is not running or the port differs.</li><li>Reference repo note: the repo uses the student's own username in the user path (replaced with angela in these notes), changes the home h1 text, and adds an extra GET /about route and a status-codes image; the five verb routes otherwise match the lesson.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l205", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains what Express middleware is (code that runs on every request before the route handlers), recaps HTML form attributes, shows how to serve a file with res.sendFile and a computed __dirname, and wires up body-parser so form data appears on req.body.</p>\n<h3>What middleware is and what it is used for</h3>\n<ul><li>Middleware is a function that sits between the incoming request and the route handlers (app.get, app.post, app.put and so on). It can inspect or change the request before a handler sees it.</li><li>Typical jobs: pre-processing requests shared by several handlers, logging (method, timing, status), authentication/authorisation checks before the request reaches back-end logic, and detecting or handling errors early.</li><li>Registered with app.use(...) above the routes, so it runs first for every matching request.</li></ul>\n<h3>HTML form recap (needed for the exercise)</h3>\n<ul><li>&lt;form action=&quot;/submit&quot; method=&quot;POST&quot;&gt;: action is the route on the server that receives the data; method is the HTTP verb used (POST here, since the form sends data to the server).</li><li>&lt;label&gt; describes the input that follows it; the for attribute links it to an input by id.</li><li>&lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; name=&quot;street&quot;&gt;: type picks the control (text, radio, checkbox, submit, etc.); name is the key under which the typed value is sent, producing key/value pairs.</li><li>required blocks submission until the field is filled in.</li><li>&lt;input type=&quot;submit&quot; value=&quot;Submit&quot;&gt; renders the button; value is the button's label.</li><li>Submitting a form like this sends an application/x-www-form-urlencoded body, e.g. street=Aberdeen&amp;pet=rabbit.</li></ul>\n<h3>Serving a file with res.sendFile and computing __dirname</h3>\n<ul><li>Static files (HTML, CSS, images) live in a public folder. Instead of res.send(string), use res.sendFile(absolutePath) to return a whole file.</li><li>sendFile needs an absolute path. Hard-coding one breaks once the server is hosted elsewhere, so compute it at runtime.</li><li>In ES modules there is no built-in __dirname. Rebuild it: import { dirname } from 'path'; import { fileURLToPath } from 'url'; const __dirname = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));</li><li>import.meta.url is the file URL of the current module; fileURLToPath turns it into a normal OS path; dirname strips the file name, leaving the folder.</li><li>Then: res.sendFile(__dirname + '/public/index.html'). The path adapts to Mac, Windows, Linux or a cloud host.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Newer Node versions also offer import.meta.dirname, and Express supports res.sendFile(path, { root: ... }) as another way to avoid string concatenation.</li></ul>\n<h3>body-parser middleware</h3>\n<ul><li>Install with npm i body-parser (already in the starter package.json, but needed when building from scratch), then import bodyParser from 'body-parser'.</li><li>Add it with app.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true })). urlencoded selects the parser for HTML form data. The extended option is required; it chooses which library parses the string behind the scenes (true = qs, supports nested objects; false = querystring). Using true is the near-universal choice.</li><li>The effect: every request gains a body property. Without the middleware req.body is undefined.</li><li>Put the logic in a POST handler, not GET: app.post('/submit', (req, res) =&gt; { console.log(req.body); }). GET only returns resources; POST receives data. The path must match the form action exactly, including case.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The sample handler never responds, so the browser keeps loading. Add res.send(...) or res.sendStatus(200) to finish the request.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Express 4.16+ includes express.urlencoded() built in, which does the same job; the reference solution1.js uses it.</li></ul>\n<h3>Testing with Postman and the browser</h3>\n<ul><li>Run with nodemon index1.js (the folder has index1-4 files, one per sub-exercise).</li><li>In Postman: method POST, URL localhost:3000/submit, Body tab, x-www-form-urlencoded, add any key/value pairs, Send. The console prints them as an object.</li><li>Removing the body-parser line and sending again prints undefined, proving the middleware is what creates req.body.</li><li>In the browser, open localhost:3000, fill the Band Name Generator form (street name and pet name), submit, and the terminal shows { street: ..., pet: ... }.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">ES module __dirname and sendFile</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\nimport { dirname } from &quot;path&quot;;\nimport { fileURLToPath } from &quot;url&quot;;\n\nconst __dirname = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.sendFile(__dirname + &quot;/public/index.html&quot;);\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Form HTML sent to /submit</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;form action=&quot;/submit&quot; method=&quot;POST&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;label for=&quot;street&quot;&gt;Street Name:&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;input placeholder=&quot;Enter Street Name&quot; type=&quot;text&quot; name=&quot;street&quot; required /&gt;\n  &lt;label for=&quot;pet&quot;&gt;Pet Name:&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;input placeholder=&quot;Enter Pet Name&quot; type=&quot;text&quot; name=&quot;pet&quot; required /&gt;\n  &lt;input type=&quot;submit&quot; value=&quot;Submit&quot; /&gt;\n&lt;/form&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">index1 target: body-parser plus POST route</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import bodyParser from &quot;body-parser&quot;;\n\napp.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true }));\n\napp.post(&quot;/submit&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  console.log(req.body); // { street: 'Aberdeen', pet: 'rabbit' }\n});</pre>\n<h2>The task: 3.4 Middleware, index1.js: log submitted form data</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Download the exercise zip, extract, cd into it, run npm install. index1.js already has the express, path/url and body-parser imports plus __dirname and a GET route that sends public/index.html; the POST route is missing or incomplete.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Submitting the Band Name Generator form should print the entered street and pet values in the terminal via req.body.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Open the project folder, run npm install, and start with nodemon index1.js.</li><li>Install body-parser (npm i body-parser) if not already in dependencies and import it.</li><li>Register app.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true })) before the routes.</li><li>Add app.post('/submit', ...) whose handler console.logs req.body.</li><li>Open localhost:3000, fill both fields, press Submit, and check the terminal output.</li><li>Optionally comment out the app.use line to confirm req.body becomes undefined.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Terminal shows an object such as { street: 'Aberdeen', pet: 'rabbit' } after each submit.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\nimport bodyParser from &quot;body-parser&quot;;\nimport { dirname } from &quot;path&quot;;\nimport { fileURLToPath } from &quot;url&quot;;\n\nconst __dirname = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\n\napp.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true }));\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.sendFile(__dirname + &quot;/public/index.html&quot;);\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/submit&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  console.log(req.body);\n});\n\napp.listen(port, () =&gt; {\n  console.log(`Listening on port ${port}`);\n});</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Registering app.use(bodyParser...) after the routes, so req.body is undefined in handlers.</li><li>Form action and app.post path differing in spelling or case.</li><li>Missing name attributes on inputs, so no keys appear in req.body.</li><li>Forgetting to extract the zip or run npm install in the right folder.</li><li>Handler never sends a response, so the browser hangs.</li><li>Reference repo note: public/index.html contains a personal footer from the repo author; ignore it.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l206", "<p class=\"summary\">Covers the morgan logging middleware, then how to write custom middleware (a function with req, res, next), why next() and registration order matter, and finishes with the Band Name Generator exercise that combines body-parser, sendFile and a custom middleware.</p>\n<h3>Middleware categories</h3>\n<ul><li>Four broad kinds: pre-processing (body-parser belongs here), logging (morgan), authentication, and error handling.</li><li>All are attached the same way: app.use(...) before the route handlers.</li></ul>\n<h3>morgan: request logging</h3>\n<ul><li>Package name morgan, roughly 4 million weekly npm downloads. Install with npm i morgan, import morgan from 'morgan', then app.use(morgan('format')).</li><li>It runs before the route handler, so every request is logged: method, URL, status code, response time and (depending on format) date, user agent, content length and so on.</li><li>Predefined formats: combined, common, dev, short, tiny. Combined is the most verbose (Apache-style, includes client and browser info); tiny prints just method, URL, status, content length and response time.</li><li>Because middleware runs first, a POST to a route with no handler is still logged, with status 404. Logging does not depend on the route existing.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The morgan docs show require(); with ES modules use import instead, as the course does.</li></ul>\n<h3>Writing a custom middleware</h3>\n<ul><li>A middleware is a function with three parameters: req, res, next. Express supplies all three.</li><li>Inside it you can read or modify req and res. When finished, call next() to hand control to the next middleware, or to the matching route handler if none are left.</li><li>req.method gives the HTTP verb (GET, POST) and req.url gives the requested path; together they make a minimal logger.</li><li>Write the function separately and pass it by name, app.use(logger), so it looks like app.use(bodyParser...) or app.use(morgan(...)). Pass the function itself, without calling it. (Extra) Library middleware such as morgan('tiny') is a factory call that returns a function; your own plain middleware is passed uncalled.</li><li>If next() is never called and no response is sent, the request hangs: the logs appear but the client never gets an answer.</li><li>Order matters: middleware runs top to bottom in the order of the app.use calls. Put anything that depends on earlier work (e.g. authentication or parsing) after the middleware that provides it.</li></ul>\n<h3>Band Name Generator walkthrough (index4 / solution4)</h3>\n<ul><li>Reuse from index1: the path/url imports and __dirname, the GET / route with res.sendFile, and the urlencoded body-parser line. Break the job into steps and test each (first verify the page is served, then the parsing).</li><li>Custom middleware bandNameGenerator reads req.body.street and req.body.pet, concatenates them into a module-level variable bandName, logs the body, then calls next().</li><li>Register it with app.use(bandNameGenerator) after the body-parser line. The POST /submit handler then sends &lt;h1&gt;Your band name is:&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;h2&gt;bandName + emoji&lt;/h2&gt; via res.send.</li><li>Putting the logic straight in the POST handler works too and is shorter; the separate middleware is just a demonstration.</li><li>Swapping the order so the generator runs before body-parser gives an error about reading properties of undefined, since req.body does not exist yet. This proves the order rule.</li><li>Copy-pasting earlier code is fine as long as you understand what it does.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">morgan with the tiny format (index2 solution style)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\nimport morgan from &quot;morgan&quot;;\n\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\n\napp.use(morgan(&quot;tiny&quot;)); // try &quot;combined&quot;, &quot;dev&quot;, &quot;short&quot;, &quot;common&quot;\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.send(&quot;Hello&quot;);\n});\n\napp.listen(port, () =&gt; console.log(`Listening on port ${port}`));</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Custom logger middleware</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function logger(req, res, next) {\n  console.log(&quot;Request method: &quot;, req.method);\n  console.log(&quot;Request URL: &quot;, req.url);\n  next(); // omit this and every request hangs\n}\n\napp.use(logger);</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Band name middleware plus POST route</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var bandName = &quot;&quot;;\n\napp.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true }));\n\nfunction bandNameGenerator(req, res, next) {\n  console.log(req.body);\n  bandName = req.body[&quot;street&quot;] + req.body[&quot;pet&quot;];\n  next();\n}\napp.use(bandNameGenerator);\n\napp.post(&quot;/submit&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.send(`&lt;h1&gt;Your band name is:&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;h2&gt;${bandName}✌️&lt;/h2&gt;`);\n});</pre>\n<h2>The task: Mini-challenges: morgan, custom logger, Band Name Generator</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Exercise 3.4 Middleware folder: index2.js (express + morgan skeleton), index3.js (plain server with no logger), index4.js (basic server, imports missing). Each has a matching solution file.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> (a) Log requests with morgan using a chosen format. (b) Write a logger middleware that prints req.method and req.url. (c) Build the Band Name Generator that replies with street name plus pet name.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>index2.js: npm install morgan if needed, import it, and add app.use(morgan('tiny')) (or another format) above the routes. Test with GET / and a POST to /submit in Postman and read the log lines.</li><li>index3.js: define function logger(req, res, next) that console.logs req.method and req.url and calls next(). Register with app.use(logger). Test GET and POST. Then remove next() once to see the hang.</li><li>index4.js: import express, path/url helpers and body-parser; recreate __dirname; serve public/index.html on GET /.</li><li>Add app.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true })).</li><li>On POST /submit respond with an h1 and h2 showing street + pet; optionally move the concatenation into a custom middleware registered after the parser.</li><li>Run solution4.js to compare behaviour; check index.html field names are street and pet.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Morgan prints a line per request; the logger prints method and URL; submitting the form shows 'Your band name is:' followed by the combined words and an emoji.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\nimport bodyParser from &quot;body-parser&quot;;\nimport { dirname } from &quot;path&quot;;\nimport { fileURLToPath } from &quot;url&quot;;\n\nconst __dirname = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\nvar bandName = &quot;&quot;;\n\napp.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true }));\n\nfunction bandNameGenerator(req, res, next) {\n  console.log(req.body);\n  bandName = req.body[&quot;street&quot;] + req.body[&quot;pet&quot;];\n  next();\n}\napp.use(bandNameGenerator);\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.sendFile(__dirname + &quot;/public/index.html&quot;);\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/submit&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.send(`&lt;h1&gt;Your band name is:&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;h2&gt;${bandName}✌️&lt;/h2&gt;`);\n});\n\napp.listen(port, () =&gt; {\n  console.log(`Listening on port ${port}`);\n});</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting next() in custom middleware, so requests never finish.</li><li>Placing the band-name middleware before body-parser, so req.body is undefined.</li><li>Storing the band name in a shared module variable (as in the solution) is unsafe with concurrent users. (Extra) Use res.locals or req.bandName instead.</li><li>Calling app.use(logger()) instead of app.use(logger).</li><li>Reference repo diverges slightly: solution2 uses the combined format rather than tiny, and public/index.html has a personal footer to ignore.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l207", "<p class=\"summary\">Final project of the Express section: build a password-gated page. A form posts a password to /check; the correct one reveals secret.html, a wrong one returns the user to the home page. A walkthrough shows a middleware-based solution.</p>\n<h3>Project structure</h3>\n<ul><li>Starter folder: an empty index.js (all code goes here) and a public folder with two pre-built pages: index.html (h1 plus a password form) and secret.html (the protected content).</li><li>Dependencies are body-parser and express. Running node solution.js without having run npm i gives ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND.</li><li>Inspect the form first: its action is /check, method POST, and the password input's name attribute is password. That name is the key to read from req.body.</li><li>The solution's password is ILoveProgramming (case-sensitive).</li></ul>\n<h3>How the solution works</h3>\n<ul><li>Imports express, body-parser, dirname and fileURLToPath, then rebuilds __dirname exactly as in earlier lessons. These lines can be copied from earlier projects; the point is to understand them, not memorise them.</li><li>A flag variable userIsAuthorised starts as false.</li><li>app.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true })) comes first so req.body exists.</li><li>passwordCheck(req, res, next) is a custom middleware: it reads req.body['password'], compares it to the secret, sets the flag to true on a match, and always calls next(). Putting the check in middleware is a design choice, treating it as authorisation before the handler; doing it inside the route handler is equally valid.</li><li>GET / sends public/index.html using res.sendFile(__dirname + ...).</li><li>POST /check sends public/secret.html if authorised; otherwise it sends the home page again or calls res.redirect('/').</li><li>res.redirect(url) tells the browser to go to another URL, giving a shorter way to return the user to the home page.</li><li>Express now bundles the urlencoded parser, so app.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: true })) replaces body-parser with no extra package. The instructor keeps body-parser because it is what most online code uses and it names what is happening.</li></ul>\n<h3>Learning advice given</h3>\n<ul><li>Try the project before looking at the solution; struggling is how the skill sticks.</li><li>Programming is about knowing where to look things up, not memorising syntax.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Password gate with custom middleware (solution)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\nimport bodyParser from &quot;body-parser&quot;;\nimport { dirname } from &quot;path&quot;;\nimport { fileURLToPath } from &quot;url&quot;;\n\nconst __dirname = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\nvar userIsAuthorised = false;\n\napp.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true }));\n\nfunction passwordCheck(req, res, next) {\n  const password = req.body[&quot;password&quot;];\n  if (password === &quot;ILoveProgramming&quot;) {\n    userIsAuthorised = true;\n  }\n  next();\n}\napp.use(passwordCheck);\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.sendFile(__dirname + &quot;/public/index.html&quot;);\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/check&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  if (userIsAuthorised) {\n    res.sendFile(__dirname + &quot;/public/secret.html&quot;);\n  } else {\n    res.redirect(&quot;/&quot;); // or res.sendFile(index.html)\n  }\n});\n\napp.listen(port, () =&gt; {\n  console.log(`Listening on port ${port}`);\n});</pre>\n<h2>The task: Secrets Access Project</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Extracted Secrets Project zip: blank index.js, public/index.html (password form posting to /check), public/secret.html.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Home page shows the form. Submitting the correct password shows secret.html; a wrong password sends the user back to the home page.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>cd into the project and run npm i (express and body-parser).</li><li>Read index.html and note the form action (/check), method (POST) and input name (password).</li><li>In index.js import express, body-parser and the path/url helpers; set up __dirname, app and port 3000.</li><li>Add the urlencoded body parser middleware.</li><li>Write a middleware (or inline check) that compares req.body.password to your chosen secret and records the result; end with next().</li><li>Add GET / serving public/index.html.</li><li>Add POST /check that sends secret.html when authorised, otherwise redirects or re-sends index.html.</li><li>Add app.listen, run with node index.js or nodemon, and test right and wrong passwords at localhost:3000.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Correct password: the secrets list page. Wrong password: back at the Secrets home page with the form.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\nimport bodyParser from &quot;body-parser&quot;;\nimport { dirname } from &quot;path&quot;;\nimport { fileURLToPath } from &quot;url&quot;;\n\nconst __dirname = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\nvar userIsAuthorised = false;\n\napp.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true }));\n\nfunction passwordCheck(req, res, next) {\n  const password = req.body[&quot;password&quot;];\n  if (password === &quot;ILoveProgramming&quot;) {\n    userIsAuthorised = true;\n  }\n  next();\n}\napp.use(passwordCheck);\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.sendFile(__dirname + &quot;/public/index.html&quot;);\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/check&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  if (userIsAuthorised) {\n    res.sendFile(__dirname + &quot;/public/secret.html&quot;);\n  } else {\n    res.redirect(&quot;/&quot;); // or res.sendFile(index.html)\n  }\n});\n\napp.listen(port, () =&gt; {\n  console.log(`Listening on port ${port}`);\n});</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Wrong name attribute: req.body key must match the input's name.</li><li>Declaring the body parser after the password middleware, so req.body is undefined.</li><li>Forgetting next() in the middleware.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The solution's userIsAuthorised flag is global and never resets, so after one correct login every later request, from any user, counts as authorised. A per-request check avoids this.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Sending a password in plain text with a text input is fine for a demo only; real apps use type=password, HTTPS and hashing.</li><li>Reference repo note: its index.js is a student's variant (password constant checked inside the route, repeated app.use lines at the end, no app.listen); solution.js is the one matching the lecture. Ignore the author footers in the HTML.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l208", "<p class=\"summary\">Study tip: explaining concepts to others and inventing your own analogies turns working-but-fuzzy understanding into durable knowledge.</p>\n<h3>Key takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li>Code that merely runs can hide gaps. Having to explain why it works, or why one approach beats another, exposes what is not yet understood and prompts further reading.</li><li>Putting an idea into words (spoken or written) forces recall and fixes it in memory.</li><li>Once a concept is clear, devise personal analogies for it rather than relying only on the course's.</li><li>Share explanations and analogies with other learners in the course Q&amp;A; a different framing may help someone else.</li><li>Regularly try to explain what you have learned to another person.</li></ul>");
